.TH TERM-NOTIFY-SOUND 1 "2026-11-18" "tmux-tools" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
term-notify-sound \- emit a terminal-aware completion notification
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B term-notify-sound
.RB [ \-\-owner
.IR pid ]
.RB [ \-\-kind
.IR kind ]
.RB [ \-\-exit-status
.IR code ]
.br
.B term-notify-sound \-\-audit
.RB [ \-\-since
.IR minutes ]
.RB [ \-\-raw ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B term-notify-sound
plays a lightweight terminal notification by emitting one standard terminal
bell. The terminal client renders that bell, so local and remote-development
sessions use the same transport.
.PP
If
.I /dev/tty
is unavailable, the helper attempts to write to the current pane TTY.
.PP
Inside tmux, the helper also marks the pane that rang as pending with
.BR "term-pane-mark set pending" ,
which sets the pane user option
.B @term_notify_pending
to the kind of attention,
.B done
unless
.B \-\-kind
says otherwise.
tmux records a bell against a window, not the pane that rang it, so this
option is how a tmux configuration can show which pane wants attention. The
helper only sets the option; the configuration that renders it owns clearing
it, for example from
.B pane-focus-in
and
.B pane-focus-out
hooks.
.PP
When the bell goes straight to the controlling terminal,
.BR term-pane-mark (1)
decides which pane, if any, this process owns. When the bell falls back to the
pane's own pty, that pane is marked directly. The helper runs the
.B term-pane-mark
beside it, falling back to the one on
.BR PATH ;
a missing helper or a failed mark is ignored.
.SS Call log
Every call that gets past argument checking appends one tab-separated line to
the call log, after the bell, so a stray bell can be traced to whatever rang
it. The fields are epoch seconds, UTC time, the route
.RB ( tty
for the terminal,
.B pane
for the pane fallback,
.B none
when nothing rang), the kind, the exit status, the owner, the
.B TMUX_PANE
of the caller, its tmux server as the socket and server pid from
.BR TMUX ,
the caller's ancestor processes by program name, nearest first and joined by
.BR < ,
and the direct caller's command line, cut to 200 characters with control
characters turned into spaces. A field with no value is
.BR \- .
Common shells and interpreters are named by the script they run, so a hook
run as
.B bash \-e /path/agent-hook-stop
reads as
.BR agent-hook-stop ;
an inline
.B sh \-c
script keeps the shell's name, and its text is in the command line field.
Up to six ancestors are recorded.
.PP
The log is created private (mode 0600, in a directory created 0700) and is
rotated to
.I \&.1
once it passes 1 MiB, so it normally keeps about 2 MiB at most. A lock keeps
two calls from rotating at once; a lock left by a call that died is cleared
once the log reaches 2 MiB. The log can hold the start
of a caller's command line, arguments included. Logging is best effort: when
the log cannot be written the call still succeeds silently.
.PP
Only bells rung through this command are logged; a bell another program
writes to the terminal leaves no line. A caller that starts the command in
the background and exits first is logged with the process that adopted it,
such as
.BR systemd ,
as its parent. A call over ssh logs on the remote host.
.PP
.B \-\-audit
reads the log, and the rotated copy, and prints the well-formed calls of the
last 60 minutes grouped by pane, server, route, kind and ancestor chain,
busiest first, with each group's count, latest time and latest command line.
A pane is also shown as
.IR session : window . pane
when its server, by socket and pid, still runs, since pane ids restart with
every server. With logging off it says so.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-owner " pid"
Pass
.I pid
to the pending mark, which then lands only while that process runs in the
foreground of the pane's terminal; see
.BR term-pane-mark (1).
An agent's hook passes the agent, so a program the agent launched, which
inherits its pane but rings through the pane fallback without a terminal of
its own, cannot mark the agent's pane. The bell still rings.
.TP
.BI \-\-kind " kind"
Pass the kind of attention,
.BR done ,
.B waiting
or
.BR blocked ,
to the pending mark; see
.BR term-pane-mark (1).
Any other kind is a usage error and rings nothing. Without this option the
mark is
.BR done .
.TP
.BI \-\-exit-status " code"
Pass a decimal shell exit status from 0 through 255 to the pending mark.
Options may appear in either order. Leading zeroes are accepted and removed.
A nonzero status publishes the pane option
.B @term_notify_exit_status
before marking the pane pending. A zero status or no status clears any stale
failure metadata. The terminal bell is unchanged; the tmux configuration
renders the status and clears it along with the pending mark when visited.
Status is accepted only through this option, not through the environment.
It is valid only with kind
.BR done .
.TP
.B \-\-audit
Summarize the call log instead of ringing; see
.BR "Call log" .
It must come first.
.TP
.BI \-\-since " minutes"
With
.BR \-\-audit ,
cover the last
.I minutes
instead of 60: a decimal number of at most nine digits.
.TP
.B \-\-raw
With
.BR \-\-audit ,
print the window's well-formed log lines unchanged instead of the summary.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TERM_NOTIFY_TTY
Override the primary terminal device, which defaults to
.IR /dev/tty .
The tmux pane TTY remains the fallback when this device cannot be opened.
.TP
.B TERM_NOTIFY_LOG
Absolute path of the call log. Set it empty, or to a relative path, to turn
logging off. Without it the log is
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/tmux-tools/term-notify-sound.log ,
or
.I ~/.local/state/tmux-tools/term-notify-sound.log
when
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
is unset; with neither variable nor
.B HOME
set, nothing is logged.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The notification was emitted or intentionally ignored after best-effort
fallbacks, or the audit ran.
.TP
.B 64
The arguments were invalid; nothing rang.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR term-pane-mark (1),
.BR tmux-copy-last-output (1)
